<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Mu'ayyad al-Dawla</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">d. 983</span> · <a href="/centuries/10th-century">10th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Mu'ayyad al-Dawla was a Buyid ruler, a son of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rukn-al-dawla">Rukn al-Dawla</a>, who governed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/isfahan">Isfahan</a> from 976 as a loyal ally of his brother <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/adud-al-dawla">Adud al-Dawla</a>. In 980 he drove out his other brother Fakhr al-Dawla and took <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hamadan">Hamadan</a> and Ray, and he then expelled the Ziyarid ruler <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/qabus">Qabus</a>, who had sheltered Fakhr al-Dawla, from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gorgan">Gurgan</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tabaristan">Tabaristan</a>. His <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vizier">vizier</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/al-sahib-ibn-abbad">al-Sahib ibn Abbad</a> was a leading patron of scholars.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/muayyad-al-dawla.jpg" alt="Mu'ayyad al-Dawla" width="320" height="159" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Classical Numismatic Group; [1] · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Coin_of_the_Buyid_ruler_Mu%27ayyad_al-Dawla,_Isfahan_mint.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
